{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "pitchzazz: real-time audio engineering walkthrough",
  "description": "Eight stops demonstrating real-time-systems understanding, DSP correctness debugging, and a controlled two-language performance comparison. Each step is short enough to say out loud during a live demo; the last line of each points at the doc section with the full depth for anyone reading async. Steps use both a line-number guess and a text `pattern` CodeTour can use to relocate the step if the surrounding file has changed since this tour was written. Three steps include a diagram (`.tours/assets/*.svg`) for the mechanisms that are easier to see than to narrate.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "1. Never block, allocate, or lock on the audio thread",
      "file": "crates/pitch-cli/src/main.rs",
      "line": 242,
      "pattern": "Input callback: copy into the ring buffer",
      "description": "This is the actual audio-input callback: copy into a lock-free ring buffer and return. Nothing else. The original prototype broke this rule three ways in ten lines — a mutex lock, a blocking channel send, and a per-callback heap allocation, all inside the callback.\n\n![Producer/consumer pipeline: audio callbacks only push/pop a lock-free ring buffer; the worker thread does all the real work](assets/realtime-safety.svg)\n\nFull story: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s \"What the original prototype got wrong\" section."
    },
    {
      "title": "2. Auditing `ringbuf`'s lock-free claim",
      "file": "crates/pitch-cli/src/main.rs",
      "line": 176,
      "pattern": "HeapRb::<f32>::new(ring_capacity).split()",
      "description": "Before trusting this line, `ringbuf`'s actual source was read (not its docs) to confirm `.split()` really gives lock-free atomic-index Producer/Consumer halves, not a mutex in disguise. That's the standing rule here: audit any dependency's real source before letting it run on the audio thread.\n\nFull story: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s Dependency Audit section."
    },
    {
      "title": "3. The bug that stalled the original project",
      "file": "crates/pitch-core/src/scale.rs",
      "line": 51,
      "pattern": "pub fn nearest_in_scale_midi",
      "description": "The original prototype's pitch detection and shifting both worked — this is the one part that was silently wrong. It hand-built a note lookup table sized to assumptions that didn't hold for every scale, corrupting some scales' \"nearest note\" results with no error.\n\nThe fix: read the scale's own notes directly instead of reconstructing them — fewer invariants to get wrong. Full story: this file's module doc above."
    },
    {
      "title": "4. Decoupling DSP block size from the host's callback size",
      "file": "crates/pitch-cli/src/main.rs",
      "line": 201,
      "pattern": "if filled < BLOCK_SIZE",
      "description": "The pitch detector needs a fixed block size; audio hosts don't guarantee one. This accumulates whatever the host hands the callback into a fixed-size buffer and only runs one correction pass once exactly `BLOCK_SIZE` samples are ready — decoupling the two.\n\nThe C++/JUCE port applies the identical pattern (`CorrectorWorker.cpp`'s `analysisBuffer`). Full story: this file's module doc."
    },
    {
      "title": "5. Perf investigation: budget miss to measured fix",
      "file": "docs/PERFORMANCE_LOG.md",
      "line": 33,
      "pattern": "Measured `PitchCorrector::process()` time",
      "description": "First live run: 40-60% over the real-time budget. Per-stage timing (not a single top-line number) showed it was entirely the pitch-shift stage — a leftover oversampling factor of 32 that had never been independently measured. Dropping it to 8 fixed it with margin.\n\nFull story: the four chained entries starting here, ending in a measured (not estimated) end-to-end latency number."
    },
    {
      "title": "6. Two ways to shift pitch: phase vocoder vs. TD-PSOLA",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/PitchEngineRegistry.cpp",
      "line": 9,
      "pattern": "const std::vector<EngineDescriptor>& availableEngines()",
      "description": "Both algorithms are registered right here, hot-swappable at runtime. They solve the same problem in genuinely different domains — one reasons about frequency, the other about time.\n\n![Phase vocoder shifts pitch by moving FFT bins, with latency fixed to the analysis window; TD-PSOLA shifts pitch by re-spacing time-domain grains at pitch marks, with latency bounded but pitch-dependent](assets/pitch-shift-algorithms.svg)\n\nThe measured payoff: TD-PSOLA's latency is ~19-46% lower depending on rate, because it scales with pitch period, not a fixed sample count. The cost: a real, disclosed reconstruction artifact on non-stationary audio — full trade-off in `docs/PERFORMANCE_LOG.md`'s TD-PSOLA entries and `docs/FINDINGS.md` #18-20."
    },
    {
      "title": "7. Hot-swap architecture + the 20-30x illusion",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/CorrectorWorker.cpp",
      "line": 87,
      "pattern": "void CorrectorWorker::requestEngineSwap",
      "description": "A new pitch-correction engine (Rust, C++ phase vocoder, or C++ TD-PSOLA) is handed off via one atomic pointer exchange, picked up between blocks, never mid-`process()`.\n\n![Hot-swap: a new engine is stored into one atomic pointer, picked up by the worker thread between blocks, then blended in via a crossfade over four blocks](assets/hot-swap.svg)\n\nThe first swap discarded the outgoing engine's warmed-up internal state, causing an audible glitch no unit test or plugin validator caught — `docs/FINDINGS.md` #14. Separately: a first-pass Rust-vs-C++ benchmark looked 20-30x apart. It was a debug-vs-release build mismatch, not a language difference — full numbers in `docs/COMPARISON.md`.\n\nThe lesson stuck: MIDI vocoder mode's \"silence\" fallback later needed the identical discipline — an early draft muted output with a hard per-block cut, which would have reintroduced this exact discontinuity class. Fixed *before* it ever shipped, not found-and-fixed again, with a per-sample gain ramp instead of a block-boundary cut."
    },
    {
      "title": "8. One validator isn't enough",
      "file": "docs/FINDINGS.md",
      "line": 28,
      "pattern": "juce::Thread` destructor asserts (UB) if destroyed while still running",
      "description": "This row: a real crash bug that passed PluginVal at strictness 5 cleanly, then was caught by Apple's own `auval` — run specifically because relying on one validator felt insufficient. Direct evidence two independently-implemented tools catch genuinely different bug classes.\n\nStill true a session later: finding #22 (MIDI vocoder mode) is the same lesson recurring in mirror image — an AU component-type flag (`aufx` vs. `aumf`) went stale after adding MIDI input, `pluginval` had no per-format concept to catch it, `auval` flagged it as an explicit warning even while still reporting an overall pass.\n\nFull ledger: every other row in this table, plus the tooling breakdown further down."
    }
  ]
}
